Step 1 — Inspect both tables
Run this in SSMS. It only reads metadata; it doesn't modify anything.
SELECT
    TABLE_NAME,
    ORDINAL_POSITION,
    COLUMN_NAME,
    DATA_TYPE,
    IS_NULLABLE
FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS
WHERE TABLE_NAME IN ('JobQueue', 'JobHistory')
ORDER BY TABLE_NAME, ORDINAL_POSITION;


This will show us the available columns, including possible job identifiers, chain names, error messages, and timestamps.
Step 2 — Check a few sample records
Once we know the column names, we'll inspect a small number of rows from each table.

For now, you can run:
SELECT TOP (5) *
FROM JobQueue
WHERE ReadyToExe = 'FROZEN';


And:
SELECT TOP (5) *
FROM JobHistory
ORDER BY 1 DESC;


Query 1 — Check historical matches
Run this read-only query in SSMS:
WITH FrozenJobs AS
(
    SELECT UID_Job, JobChainName
    FROM dbo.JobQueue
    WHERE Ready2EXE = 'FROZEN'
),
HistoryMatch AS
(
    SELECT
        f.UID_Job,
        COUNT(h.UID_JobHistory) AS HistoryRecords,
        SUM(CASE WHEN h.ErrorMessages IS NOT NULL
                  AND LTRIM(RTRIM(h.ErrorMessages)) <> ''
                 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS HistoryRecordsWithErrors,
        MAX(h.EndedAt) AS LatestHistoricalEnd
    FROM FrozenJobs f
    LEFT JOIN dbo.JobHistory h
        ON h.UID_Job = f.UID_Job
    GROUP BY f.UID_Job
)
SELECT
    COUNT(*) AS CurrentFrozenJobs,
    SUM(CASE WHEN HistoryRecords > 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)
        AS JobsWithHistoryMatches,
    SUM(CASE WHEN HistoryRecords = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)
        AS JobsWithoutHistoryMatches,
    SUM(HistoryRecords) AS MatchedHistoryRecords,
    SUM(HistoryRecordsWithErrors) AS HistoricalRecordsWithErrors
FROM HistoryMatch;


This gives us a quick overview without returning the actual error messages.
Query 2 — Inspect a few matched records
SELECT TOP (20)
    q.UID_Job,
    q.JobChainName,
    q.TaskName,
    q.Ready2EXE,
    q.StartAt AS CurrentStartAt,
    h.UID_JobHistory,
    h.WasError,
    h.StartAt AS HistoricalStartAt,
    h.EndedAt AS HistoricalEndedAt,
    CASE
        WHEN h.ErrorMessages IS NULL
          OR LTRIM(RTRIM(h.ErrorMessages)) = ''
        THEN 'No error text'
        ELSE 'Error text available'
    END AS ErrorTextAvailability,
    LEN(h.ErrorMessages) AS ErrorTextLength
FROM dbo.JobQueue q
LEFT JOIN dbo.JobHistory h
    ON h.UID_Job = q.UID_Job
WHERE q.Ready2EXE = 'FROZEN'
ORDER BY q.XDateUpdated DESC;


This lets us see whether the same job has historical records and whether those records contain error text, without displaying the error contents.

3. Our next task: establish historical error coverage
Before involving AI, let's run one query to determine how much useful historical evidence exists per job chain.
SELECT
    JobChainName,
    COUNT(*) AS HistoricalRecords,
    SUM(CASE WHEN WasError = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)
        AS RecordsMarkedAsError,
    SUM(
        CASE
            WHEN ErrorMessages IS NOT NULL
             AND LTRIM(RTRIM(ErrorMessages)) <> ''
            THEN 1 ELSE 0
        END
    ) AS RecordsWithErrorText,
    MAX(EndedAt) AS LatestHistoricalEnd
FROM dbo.JobHistory
GROUP BY JobChainName
ORDER BY RecordsWithErrorText DESC, RecordsMarkedAsError DESC;


This query doesn't display the error messages themselves. It tells us which job chains have the most historical error evidence.

Next, run the corresponding query for the current frozen jobs:
SELECT
    JobChainName,
    COUNT(*) AS CurrentFrozenJobs,
    SUM(
        CASE
            WHEN ErrorMessages IS NOT NULL
             AND LTRIM(RTRIM(ErrorMessages)) <> ''
            THEN 1 ELSE 0
        END
    ) AS FrozenJobsWithErrorText,
    COUNT(DISTINCT TaskName) AS DistinctTaskNames
FROM dbo.JobQueue
WHERE Ready2EXE = 'FROZEN'
GROUP BY JobChainName
ORDER BY CurrentFrozenJobs DESC;
